  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes the pituitary–thyroid axis and the broad metabolic and cardiovascular actions of thyroid hormone. At the top, the pituitary gland releases TSH, which stimulates the thyroid gland to produce thyroid hormone, while curved arrows indicate feedback regulation between these two glands.\n\nThe lower part of the figure shows how thyroid hormone affects multiple target tissues. In the liver, it increases lipid turnover and gluconeogenesis but appears to reduce insulin sensitivity. In skeletal muscle, it raises oxidative metabolism and improves insulin sensitivity and/or glucose use. In adipose tissue, it promotes lipolysis, thermogenesis, and overall energy expenditure, highlighting its role in boosting metabolic rate.\n\nThe figure also emphasizes important cardiovascular effects. In the heart, thyroid hormone enhances diastolic function and contractile activity. In blood vessels, it lowers arterial resistance while increasing venous tone and the production of vasoactive factors such as nitric oxide and ANP. Overall, the image conveys that thyroid hormone, under pituitary control, acts as a systemic regulator linking endocrine signaling to whole-body metabolism, energy balance, and cardiovascular performance.",
